        "evidence": "Theorem 1 and subsequent remarks establish convergence to the class of unbiased k-points, but note that oscillation or non-convergence to strict global minima can occur under specific symmetric configurations.",
        "evidence_excerpt": "The k-means thus converge to some such relative position, but theorem 1 does not quite permit us to eliminate the interesting possibility that the two means oscillate slowly but indefinitely around the center. Theorem 1 provides for a.s. convergence of sum from i=1 to k of pi |xi - ui| to zero in a slightly broader class of situations. This is where the unbiased k-points x = (x1, x2, ..., xk) with xi != xj for i != j, are all stable in the sense that for each such x, W(y) >= W(x) (and hence V(y) >= V(x)) for all y in a neighborhood of x. In this case, each such x falls in one of finitely many equivalence classes such that W is constant on each class. This is illustrated by the above example, where there is only a single equivalence class.",
