          "2.3. Remarks",
          "Page 288"
        ]
      }
    ],
    "candidate_hypotheses": [
      {
        "hypothesis": "For fixed marginal distributions, the within-class variance for the optimal partition is maximal when the joint distribution is actually the product of the marginals.",
        "rationale": "Intuitive considerations and properties of independence suggest that breaking dependency structures increases within-class variance across scrambled versus unscrambled partitions.",
        "evidence": "The author proposes a related conjecture regarding the maximization of within-class variance under product distributions to support a nonparametric test for independence.",
        "evidence_excerpt": "The power of this procedure remains to be seen. On the encouraging side is the related conjecture, that for fixed marginal distributions, the within-class variance for the optimal partition as defined in section 1 is maximal when the joint distribution is actually the product of the marginals. If this is true (and it seems likely that it is, at least for a large class of reasonable distributions), then we reason that since the k-means process tends to give a good partition, this difference will be preserved in the scrambled and unscrambled variances, particularly for large samples. Variation in the within-class variance due to the random order in which the points are processed, can be reduced by taking several random orders, and averaging their result. If this is done for the scrambled runs as well, the Type I error is preserved, while the power is increased somewhat.",
        "sections": [
